WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:03.302
-Maya!
-Hi.

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.706
-Oh, it's so good to see you.
I like -- Oh, my gosh.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.474
I love your hair.
I miss seeing you.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:09.775
-Thanks, bud.
I know. I love you.

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:12.312
-Even though I just saw you
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.147
I love you, bud.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.983
Congrats on "Saturday Night
Live" last weekend.

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.485
I want to walk through
some of the steps

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:20.453
because I can't
even really imagine

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.121
all of the steps that went on.

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:25.357
First of all, it's the first
show back in a pandemic.

00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:27.527
-Yeah.
-What was it like?

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.495
I didn't want to go up
and bother anybody.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:32.031
-It -- It was nuts.

00:00:32.031 --> 00:00:33.365
I mean, you and I were there

00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:36.636
for the first show
after September 11th.

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.037
-Yeah.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.873
-And when anthrax was
in the building, I believe.

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:42.675
-Oh, yeah.
-That was another doozy.

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:44.244
-Do you remember that?

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:45.778
-That was crazy.

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:49.115
And I feel like there was
a reminiscent feeling of

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:53.386
there was -- you were in the
center of everything going on.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:58.124
And honestly, "SNL's"
been doing -- they're doing

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:00.727
an unbelievable job
of making you feel safe.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:04.630
Everyone's tested every single
time we walk in, but it's "SNL."

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.466
Everything happens
at the last minute,

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:09.869
you know, in order to get the
best show possible on the air.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:15.141
So imagine that with,
like, PPE and masks

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.109
and people spread apart.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:18.010
It's wild.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.313
It was so strange

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:24.283
and yet so comforting
because the minute

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.886
you're in that building and you
hear the music, you're like,

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.621
"Oh, life's back to
normal again."

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:29.689
-Yeah.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:32.291
-With just some
little alterations.

00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:34.127
-How was the script
and cue cards?

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:35.861
How do you deal with rehearsing?

00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:38.931
-Dude, that's also crazy.
I mean, think about it.

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:42.134
Think about our
beloved cue card crew

00:01:42.134 --> 00:01:44.370
having to write everything down
and then changes

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.740
but in a time of molasses.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.944
So they're like -- you know,
with masks everything is slower.

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:53.379
-Yeah.
-The protocol's slower.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.249
I think scripts are on iPads
right now, by the way,

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.951
which is also
like a future language

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:01.621
that you and I
never experienced.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.522
We're used to, like,
you talk to somebody.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:05.158
They write it down.
They get it to scripty.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.992
They get the changes in.
They go to cue cards.

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:12.732
I didn't see my lines that
I said on the show until air.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.301
So it was really scary.
-No way.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.736
-It was so scary.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:21.140
I was not even thinking about
what Kamala speaks like.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.675
I was just worried, like,
"Do I have pants on?

00:02:23.675 --> 00:02:28.180
Are we -- Is -- Like, is there
a booger hanging out?

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.583
Like, is everything okay?"

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.987
And then, later on, I was like,
"Well, there's the impression."

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.088
Me, like, terrified
out of my wits,

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:38.891
looking at the wrong cameras,
like I was out of my body.

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.392
-No, it was perfect.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:43.396
You had pants on and you nailed
it, by the way. It was great.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:46.732
-I'm so grateful, always,
to be a part of that place.

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:48.668
And then, like, when,
in my wildest dreams,

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.069
did I think there would
ever be a candidate

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:54.039
that looks remotely close to
whatever this is?

00:02:54.039 --> 00:02:56.676
It -- The whole thing is --
-It just worked.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:03:00.980
-And I realize, like, I started
working there 21 years ago.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.116
How am I still standing?
-Yeah.

00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:05.451
Isn't it great?
-How am I still standing?

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.286
Yeah.
-I love it because you're funny.

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.621
-I love it.

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:13.125
-When Vice President Biden
announced Kamala Harris

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:15.595
as his running mate,
you were, somehow,

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.397
on a Zoom interview thing
or something, live.

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.499
You can't write this.

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:21.901
And you're on live,

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:25.070
and somebody says,
"He just chose Kamala Harris."

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:31.044
And you were so human and so
real, your first reaction was...

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:33.445
-Was, "Oh, [bleep]."
-Yeah.

00:03:33.445 --> 00:03:36.782
-You know why? Because we're
in the middle of a pandemic.

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:38.818
-I know we are.
I know we are.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:41.487
-I was like, "How am I
gonna get to New York?

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:43.022
Oh, [bleep]."
-Yeah.

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.323
-That's complicated.

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:47.359
That's really complicated
because it's my civic duty.

00:03:47.359 --> 00:03:48.594
I got to do it.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:51.530
I got to get there
and do whatever I need to do

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.868
to make sure
that she wins this election

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.738
and also that I do a good job.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.706
And how the hell do I do that?

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:03.542
Because I haven't left my house
in seven months.

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:07.447
-You did a virtual event
with Senator Harris,

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:10.149
and Amy Poehler was there
and Hillary Clinton.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.384
-How about that?
-All at the same time.

00:04:12.384 --> 00:04:14.419
Was that your first time me--
-That was nuts.

00:04:14.419 --> 00:04:17.590
Yeah, that was the first time
I met Kamala and Hillary.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.125
We don't hang out that much.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.927
I have never met
those incredible women.

00:04:21.927 --> 00:04:25.531
And it was bananas,
and it was so cool.

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:28.334
We were just, like, chatting
about quarantine,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.769
asking Hillary about quarantine.

00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:32.672
She was like, "Ah, I've been
catching up on sleep."

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.340
Like, the coolest --
-Yeah.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.841
-"Hanging out with
my grandkids."

00:04:35.841 --> 00:04:41.213
And then poor Poehler's sound
went out and she was so upset

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:43.616
'cause, you know, she is ver--

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.285
she's a very, like,
professional lady.

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:48.021
And she was not pleased
that her sound went out

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:49.488
'cause it was not her fault.

00:04:49.488 --> 00:04:51.890
And I was trying to, like,
come up with, you know,

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:56.729
like, do some, like,
wacky shabba-shabba.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.632
And I said,
"Man, it's sabotage."

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:01.834
And right away
Hillary chimed in.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.669
She goes,
"It's probably the Russians."

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:07.006
-Oh, nice!

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.840
Score.
-For the win. Score.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.778
-She shoots, she scores.
I love it.

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:13.612
-What a stud.
-Well, speaking of scoring,

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:15.114
how about this segue?

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:18.517
You scored so hard as Kamala
that, my friend,

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:21.387
you won an Emmy
for that performance.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.521
So, congrats.

00:05:22.521 --> 00:05:23.823
I'm not the only one
who thinks it's awesome.

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.558
-Thank you. Crazy.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:26.992
-Then you won another Emmy!

00:05:26.992 --> 00:05:29.695
You won two Emmys!
-Yes.

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:32.398
I double-Emmy'd.
-You double --

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:33.899
Have you -- I know you've been
nominated before.

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:35.368
Have you ever won before?

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.204
-I've never won anything
in my life.

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:43.609
Like, I won -- I won that first
Emmy for "Big Mouth" really,

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.679
truly, genuinely, like,
not expecting anything.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.748
And then I was
nominated for "SNL."

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:52.384
And I was like,
"Ooh, now I want another one."

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:54.887
Like, I became an addict,
like, overnight.

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:55.888
-You got the bug.
-I was like,

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:57.489
"I want to win again."
-Yeah.

00:05:57.489 --> 00:05:58.924
Well, it's about to go down.
-"Give it to me again."

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:01.093
-Yeah. Give it to me again.
-Yeah.

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:03.862
-I want to talk to you about
"Hubie Halloween."

00:06:03.862 --> 00:06:05.364
-Okay.
-Alright.

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:07.533
More with Maya Rudolph
when we come back, everybody.

